Build a package from a checkout with:

    sudo apt install debhelper golang-go
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

The module requires Go 1.26 or newer. Ubuntu 26.04 ships that as golang-go;
on Debian trixie, whose golang-go is older, use a toolchain from go.dev or the
golang:1.27-trixie container image and pass `-d` to skip the build-dependency
check. The resulting package installs on either, since the binary is static.
CI builds the release packages this way (see .github/workflows/main.yaml) and
stamps debian/changelog with the version from dist/version.sh. The entry in
the repository is a placeholder, so a package built here is version 0.0.0.

The build needs network access to download the Go modules, or a vendor/
directory made with `go mod vendor`. `DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS=nocheck` skips the
tests; cross builds (`-a arm64`) skip them automatically and need binutils for
the target architecture.

After installing, initialise or enrol the node once by hand (see
/usr/share/doc/cheesecloth/README.md), put its settings in
/etc/cheesecloth/config.yaml, then `systemctl enable --now cheesecloth`.
